    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `label_ticket` in `app/labeler.py`, and `pytest -q` passes. That suite has only one test, which just checks `LABELS`. To check the actual function, I ran the real SDK client against a fake HTTP transport. It sent exactly one request with model `claude-sonnet-4-6`, `max_tokens: 5`, `temperature: 0`, the system prompt and the ticket as the user message. A reply of `\" Feature\\n\"` came back as `\"feature\"`.\n\n**`temperature` can't be passed the usual way.** The pinned `anthropic==1.8.0` no longer has a `temperature` argument on `messages.create`. Writing `temperature=0` would crash with a `TypeError` on every call. `claude-sonnet-4-6` still accepts temperature, so I send it as `extra_body={\"temperature\": 0}` and it still ends up in the request. I left a comment explaining why. This will break if `MODEL` is ever changed to a newer model like Sonnet 5 or Opus 5: those reject temperature with a 400 error.\n\nHow it works:\n- The system prompt tells the model to answer with exactly one word from the list: `bug, feature, question`. It's built from `LABELS`, so it stays in sync.\n- The reply text is lower-cased and stripped. Anything that isn't in `LABELS` becomes `\"question\"`. That includes an empty reply or a reply cut off by the 5-token limit.\n- No dependencies were added.\n\n**Leftover file:** I couldn't delete my test script `_smoke.py` in the project root because deleting needs your approval. It isn't needed, so you can remove it."
